00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0%
 
hi everyone welcome to the third  tutorial in our imaging science series  at the MIT Museum my name is Brian Myrna  and I'm the education coordinator at the  at the Museum and today we're going to  be looking at high speed photography if  you haven't done so already please check  out our earlier tutorials on camera  settings and macro photography  especially the camera settings those are  going to be referenced a lot in this  video so what we're going to be seeing  today is how do you take these images  for example the ones I have below of  like the high speed water drop the balut  water balloon popping on the right how  do you capture these images using the  photography equipment that you have to  start let's take a picture of a box fan  now for reference I have a picture of  the fan wall it's not moving and you can  see what the blades of the fan look like  now if I turn the fan on and try to  capture the fan blades wallach spinning  I'm going to end up with something like  this so in this example I use a fast  shutter speed to try to capture that fan  blade as it's moving quickly the problem  with using such a fast shutter speed is  that not much light can get into the  camera very few photons can enter the  camera and reach the center in that  short period of time so we get a dark  image now instead maybe I'm going to try  a longer shutter speed to fix that so if  I use a longer shutter speed a slower  shutter speed I now get a brighter image  but you can see now that the fan blades  are very blurry so as you take the  picture while that shutter is open I'm  going to be capturing the fan as it  moves so both the long shutter speed and  a short shutter speed aren't giving me  the image that I would like ideally I'd  like it to kind of look like as if the  fan wasn't spinning on top of that  there's another issue that we run into  and that is rolling shutter so if you  look closely at this image so this is  one taken at fast shutter speed and so  it's very dark you can see  a little bit better though because I  raised the eye so the result of doing  that is it's a very grainy image you can  see all the kind of random colors in  here but what I want you to focus on is  if you look at the distance between the  fan blade so the bottom fan blades here  look like they're farther apart than the  ones on top the fan blades are a little  distorted if you look back at our  reference image  all of these fan blades are evenly  spaced this distortion effect is known  as rolling shutter to see this a little  more clearly I'm going to try to take  the same picture but using a flash so  the flash is going to give us more light  so it'll fix part of our problem with  there not being enough light in our  picture but there's also a very strange  effect as I increase the shutter speed  so I'm starting at a three twentieth of  a second and increased to one eight  hundredths of a second to freeze that  motion to get rid of any motion blur you  can see this black line in my photo and  it's getting larger and larger this is  again due to the rolling shutter effect  so let's take a look at what causes this  and once we understand it we can figure  out how to avoid it the shutter can work  in two different modes so here you see  the center of the camera and we're going  to put the shutters over that so  normally before you take a picture the  shutter is closed and you notice there's  actually two parts of the shutter right  here and these are going to move in  different ways so when you first take a  picture at most slow shutter speeds so  usually if you're below a three  twentieth of a second so it's longer  than a three twentieth of a second so  what's gonna happen is when you hit the  shutter button one leaf of the shutter  is gonna drop down and open the shutter  and then following that the second blade  is going to close for the fraction of  time that you set  and that works again as I said up to  about three twentieth of a second once  you get faster than that it's going to  work a little bit differently so what's  going to happen here is if you do a  faster shutter seed let's say one one  thousandth of a second the shutter  instead of opening all at once is going  to open just a little bit and then both  shutters are going to move down the  center like this in a scanning motion  now you may think this might be a little  weird right different parts of the  sensor are going to be detecting photons  at different times and this is why we  see the effects of the distorted fan  blades or the bands in the images that  we just looked at for example when using  the flash in the images the flash turns  on and off very quickly so it turns on  part of the image is being recorded but  then as the shutter moves the flash is  already off so the remainder of the  image is dark now there's something you  may also be thinking is well if you're  taking a picture with a cell phone it  doesn't have a mechanical shutter it  doesn't have these pieces of plastic  that move in front of the center it's  too small and so what a cell phone will  do is in order to take a picture with a  cell phone you need to be able to see  the image before you take it there's a  live feed you can see the light coming  in to the camera but in order for you to  see a live picture before you take the  pictures you can see what you're trying  to image you need to capture photons and  compute do computations on that data and  put it on the screen very quickly the  camera is not fast enough the cell phone  is not fast enough to do that all at  once so what the cell phone actually  does is it scans just like that rolling  shutter I just showed you and it's going  to scan line by line so it's going to  record the data where this green line is  and it's going to start display  as it scans the next line and it's going  to scan and display scanned and display  and it's gonna go like this and this is  also going to cause that rolling shutter  effect the rolling shutter effect  especially in an electronic shutter also  has a very interesting effect in video  so if you watch here as I move the  camera back and forth lines that should  be parallel and vertical straight up and  down  become slanted and distorted so now that  we understand how the shutter works in  our cameras we can look at well how can  we avoid these problems in order to take  some super sharp high-speed photos so  look let's imagine we're gonna take a  picture  and we're gonna put this camera in a  completely dark room so there's no light  in this room and when we turn on our  shutter let's set our shutter speed to  be very long let's say one minute if my  camera is in a very dark room so what  what is it going to capture well even  though the shutters on for a very long  period of time one minute if there's no  light in the room we're not going to  capture anything but a dark image  because there's no light entering the  camera now during that time what if we  turned on a flashlight let's say for one  second  what are we gonna see well if we turn  that flashlight on for one second but  have a shutter speed of one minute in  the dark room we're only going to  capture a light from when that light was  on the rest of that shutter speed we're  not going to be capturing any light this  is going to allow us to avoid that  rolling shutter effect by having long  shutter speeds but also freeze things by  strategically choosing a light source  now you may be asking how do I get a  perfectly dark room and it may not be  possible to have it 100% dark but one  thing you can do is adjust your camera  settings so here what we're gonna want  to do is take our ISO and drop that as  low as possible  the lower you can get your ISO the less  ambient light from around the room  you're going to capture and the darker  the room is going to be your camera is  going to be only sensitive to that  bright light that we're going to end up  producing so in that way of a darkroom  or we've adjusted our camera settings so  we have a relatively dark room we now  need a light source and so for a light  source we want to pick something that  can turn on and off very very quickly  the amount of time that the light is on  is now going to be essentially our  shutter speed so no matter how long our  shutter is open so again let's say we  have it open for 10 seconds if our light  flashes on and off for a ten thousandth  of a second don't lean on for a tiny  tiny bit of time then that's basically  our shutter speed a ten thousandth of a  second and we can capture things very  quickly without worrying about rolling  shutter there are a few different ways  to do this the simplest is to use your  cell phone and you can actually use the  LED on the back of your phone that you  usually use as a flashlight with an app  and if you just search for strobe light  apps you can turn that led into a strobe  light so this strobe light it'll flash  on and off very quickly but what really  matters is how long that light is on and  a lot of these apps you can adjust that  and get a very short flash duration  another option is if you have a camera  flash so if you have a regular flash for  a camera and you set this to the lowest  power you're going to get a flash  duration of about one ten thousandth of  a second so now your shutter speed is  one ten thousandth of a second no matter  what the shutter speed on your camera is  actually setup if you want to go even  faster and catch things that are faster  than ten thousandth of a second or if  you're taking pictures with the  cellphone and can't set up a regular  camera flash you can also use a strobe  light you want to make sure that the  sure light has  a filament instead of LEDs the filament  flashes are on for shorter periods of  time than LED flashes but what this  strobe light does is the light turns on  and off within one millionth of a second  so using a strobe light and you can find  these online fairly cheap you can get  shutter speeds of essentially one  millionth of a second and capture things  that happen very very quickly so now  let's look at a few examples otos and  see what some example setups look like  when using strobe or high-speed  photography  so the first three photos here are water  dropped photos taken in different ways  some are with a flash summer with a  camera flash somewhere with a strobe  light they're using different cameras  and on the right here we have a popping  water balloon this one's also using a  regular camera flash for the setup what  I recommend is having the light source  at 90 degrees to where you're taking the  picture so you can see in the lower left  that we have the camera set up as a  strobe light so this black black phone  here is being used as the strobe light  and then we're taking the taking the  picture with this other light the 90  degree having them 90 degrees together  will give you a really nice exposure but  feel free to experiment with that by  changing the light angles light from  above below different angles use  different colors those are all going to  give you very different effects in the  second image on the bottom we have a  similar setup with dropping dropping  water except now we're using a DSLR with  a traditional camera flash now this is  straight on to the photo so we're going  to get a different look a different look  in our photo in terms of lighting but  this will also work in the third picture  on the bottom we are using the strobe  light and you can see in this this blue  image here on the top this water drop  image it's also cropped in its was  basically taken the same as the image on  the left but it's been cropped in so you  can see all of that detail  we've also added  food coloring and that gives a very  different look so add food coloring  tried all sorts of different experiments  here and we'd love to see what you come  up with thank you for joining us for  this tutorial in our imaging science  series with the MIT Museum and Koch  Institute we'd love to see your stunning  science photos if you'd like to share  them with us you can show them on  Facebook Twitter and Instagram and use  the hashtag MIT em imaging science to  share those with us at the end of this  program so on June 20th will be holding  a webinar to look at what makes a great  science photo and some of the so  submissions have a chance to be featured  during that webinar for more information  on the imaging science program other  tutorials and other information and  resources please visit the MIT Museum  website at the link below  and you will find a list of our other  resources as part of the Imaging Science  program

